From 90c7b1e6ff124bb515731c54b8cf6d00df8eaf56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 10 Jul 2026 00:05:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 39/66] fix(test): materialize routed rig dir so
 hasAssignedOpenWork test is macOS-hermetic (gfork-1bw)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

TestHasAssignedOpenWork_UsesPinnedBeadsDirInsteadOfRigOrRepoFlag failed on macOS
(and on pure base 81233d36 — inherited-upstream, not a fork regression). It writes
a route {prefix:gt-, path:gastown/mayor/rig} into townRoot/.beads/routes.jsonl but
never creates the gastown/mayor/rig dir on disk, then asserts hasAssignedOpenWork
pins BEADS_DIR to townRoot/gastown/mayor/rig/.beads.

Root cause is a symlink asymmetry in beads.pathWithin (internal/beads/routes.go),
reached via GetRigDirForName. pathWithin runs filepath.EvalSymlinks on both root
(townRoot) and path (the candidate rig dir), keeping the original on error. On
macOS t.TempDir() lives under /var, a symlink to /private/var: townRoot resolves to
/private/var/... but the absent rig dir fails EvalSymlinks and stays /var/..., so
filepath.Rel sees divergent prefixes, pathWithin returns false, GetRigDirForName
returns "", and hasAssignedOpenWork falls back to the ROUTING branch. BEADS_DIR
then points at townRoot/.beads instead of the pinned rig dir, the fake bd exits 1
on its BEADS_DIR guard, the lookup returns false, and the test fails at line 62.

This is a test-hermeticity defect, not a production bug: real towns keep the rig
dir on disk (so EvalSymlinks resolves both paths symmetrically) and do not live
under /var. Fix is test-only — os.MkdirAll(expectedBeadsDir) before the lookup,
which materializes the routed rig dir, keeps EvalSymlinks symmetric, and makes the
test exercise the pinned branch it was written to verify (previously it was
silently testing the routing fallback). Empirically confirmed: pathWithin flips
false->true and GetRigDirForName resolves correctly once the dir exists. Target
test fail->PASS (0.37s); full internal/daemon package green under -race (159.9s,
0 findings). No upstream PR per fork directive; the latent pathWithin robustness
gap is documented in the gfork-1bw bead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 internal/daemon/has_assigned_work_test.go | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/internal/daemon/has_assigned_work_test.go b/internal/daemon/has_assigned_work_test.go
index a2cdd5ad..7e0334fc 100644
--- a/internal/daemon/has_assigned_work_test.go
+++ b/internal/daemon/has_assigned_work_test.go
@@ -28,6 +28,18 @@ func TestHasAssignedOpenWork_UsesPinnedBeadsDirInsteadOfRigOrRepoFlag(t *testing
 	binDir := t.TempDir()
 	logPath := filepath.Join(binDir, "bd.log")
 	expectedBeadsDir := filepath.Join(townRoot, "gastown", "mayor", "rig", ".beads")
+	// Materialize the routed rig dir on disk before the lookup. hasAssignedOpenWork
+	// resolves the pinned dir via GetRigDirForName -> pathWithin, which runs
+	// filepath.EvalSymlinks on both townRoot and the candidate rig dir. On macOS
+	// t.TempDir() lives under /var (a symlink to /private/var): a rig dir that does
+	// not exist on disk stays unresolved while townRoot resolves, so the two paths
+	// diverge (/var vs /private/var), pathWithin returns false, and the daemon falls
+	// back to the routing branch instead of the pinned branch this test exercises.
+	// Creating it keeps EvalSymlinks symmetric and mirrors a real town, where the
+	// rig dir always exists on disk.
+	if err := os.MkdirAll(expectedBeadsDir, 0o755); err != nil {
+		t.Fatalf("mkdir rig beads: %v", err)
+	}
 	script := `#!/bin/sh
 for arg in "$@"; do
   case "$arg" in
